WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:01.830
- This is so crazy for me
to hear all these details

00:00:01.830 --> 00:00:03.420
'cause I've never talked
to a composer ever.

00:00:03.420 --> 00:00:05.430
So this is just so cool to get these

00:00:05.430 --> 00:00:07.020
inside look to everything.

00:00:07.020 --> 00:00:09.570
- Hi, I'm Michael Abels,
the composer of "Nope".

00:00:09.570 --> 00:00:11.280
- Yo, everybody, my name is Brandon Perea.

00:00:11.280 --> 00:00:12.750
I play Angel Torres in "Nope"

00:00:12.750 --> 00:00:15.111
- And this is "Trade Secrets".

00:00:15.111 --> 00:00:17.528
(soft music)

00:00:19.170 --> 00:00:20.250
- Michael Abels.

00:00:20.250 --> 00:00:21.510
- Brandon, great to meet you.

00:00:21.510 --> 00:00:23.250
- Great to meet you in person, finally.

00:00:23.250 --> 00:00:25.980
We've been Instagram
friends for a little while

00:00:25.980 --> 00:00:27.480
and I'm definitely a fan.

00:00:27.480 --> 00:00:29.580
So it's cool to be sitting here right now

00:00:29.580 --> 00:00:31.470
across from you and I'm
just so thankful to be here.

00:00:31.470 --> 00:00:32.850
- Thank you, likewise, likewise.

00:00:32.850 --> 00:00:35.310
I've really been enjoying
watching your performance

00:00:35.310 --> 00:00:37.950
for all these months as we
work on post production.

00:00:37.950 --> 00:00:38.783
- Thank you so much.

00:00:38.783 --> 00:00:40.350
Like, I've been a fan of you, of course,

00:00:40.350 --> 00:00:43.080
since "Get Out", "Us" and now
you're doing "Nope" as well.

00:00:43.080 --> 00:00:46.170
It's just incredible the
scores that you've crafted

00:00:46.170 --> 00:00:50.730
and I use them a lot in my social settings

00:00:50.730 --> 00:00:51.750
like when we're playing this game

00:00:51.750 --> 00:00:53.160
- Really?
- Called "Mafia"

00:00:53.160 --> 00:00:54.870
I love to play the "Get Out" score

00:00:54.870 --> 00:00:57.360
or the "Us" score 'cause it
really brings in the tone

00:00:57.360 --> 00:00:59.820
of like, this serious, spooky vibe.

00:00:59.820 --> 00:01:00.900
- Oh, that's so cool.

00:01:00.900 --> 00:01:03.210
- Yeah, so that's definitely
something I utilize

00:01:03.210 --> 00:01:05.610
in my social gatherings.
(Michael chuckling)

00:01:05.610 --> 00:01:06.443
- Wow.
- So, thank you

00:01:06.443 --> 00:01:07.980
for providing that, truly.

00:01:07.980 --> 00:01:08.880
- Absolutely.

00:01:08.880 --> 00:01:10.530
- How did you get involved
with Jordan first?

00:01:10.530 --> 00:01:12.660
I don't know the story, so I'm excited

00:01:12.660 --> 00:01:14.790
to hear how you got involved with Jordan.

00:01:14.790 --> 00:01:17.220
- I have been writing music all my life

00:01:17.220 --> 00:01:19.800
and especially music for orchestra.

00:01:19.800 --> 00:01:21.630
I wrote a lot of concert music, you know,

00:01:21.630 --> 00:01:22.650
for in the concert hall.

00:01:22.650 --> 00:01:23.850
Some of that was on YouTube.

00:01:23.850 --> 00:01:25.320
It had a couple dozen hits.

00:01:25.320 --> 00:01:27.000
One of 'em was Jordan when he was lookin'

00:01:27.000 --> 00:01:28.140
for someone to score "Get Out".

00:01:28.140 --> 00:01:29.400
And he was looking for, I think, someone

00:01:29.400 --> 00:01:32.070
who could write in really scary style

00:01:32.070 --> 00:01:34.770
but also who understood the
African American experience.

00:01:34.770 --> 00:01:38.790
So he had the producers hunt
me down and took a meeting.

00:01:38.790 --> 00:01:39.840
- That's incredible.

00:01:39.840 --> 00:01:40.740
- Isn't it?
- That was Jordan's

00:01:40.740 --> 00:01:42.540
introduction into filming.

00:01:42.540 --> 00:01:44.760
Was it your introduction
into filmmaking, as well?

00:01:44.760 --> 00:01:47.550
- I had done, you know,
some commercials and stuff

00:01:47.550 --> 00:01:49.500
in college, but had not really made

00:01:49.500 --> 00:01:50.730
any headway in the industry.

00:01:50.730 --> 00:01:52.800
So to get a call from Jordan Peele

00:01:52.800 --> 00:01:54.510
out of the blue was really a dream.

00:01:54.510 --> 00:01:56.430
- Wow, all right, 'cause
this is insane to me.

00:01:56.430 --> 00:01:58.350
I'm actually learning
stuff as we're talking.

00:01:58.350 --> 00:02:00.570
I just listened to the
score recently for "Nope"

00:02:00.570 --> 00:02:01.710
and it's another home run.

00:02:01.710 --> 00:02:04.410
And it's so different
from "Get Out" and "Us".

00:02:04.410 --> 00:02:06.630
'Cause you know, Jordan's
kind of crafted this film

00:02:06.630 --> 00:02:08.820
on a grander scale, it's very big.

00:02:08.820 --> 00:02:11.070
And I feel like you have
just done that with sound

00:02:11.070 --> 00:02:12.420
and the instruments you've chosen.

00:02:12.420 --> 00:02:13.253
- [Michael] Well, thank you.

00:02:13.253 --> 00:02:17.010
- It's so great to get a new kind of style

00:02:17.010 --> 00:02:19.980
and edge from you, and also
where you still sprinkle

00:02:19.980 --> 00:02:22.290
in, like, your little things that you do

00:02:22.290 --> 00:02:24.390
with like, the little,
the shadow of sounds

00:02:24.390 --> 00:02:25.890
of (whispering) that's a, he's a.

00:02:25.890 --> 00:02:27.277
Like, I hear a couple
of those and I'm like,

00:02:27.277 --> 00:02:28.800
"Oh, that's Abel's man."

00:02:28.800 --> 00:02:30.630
But then I'm like, "But
this is so different."

00:02:30.630 --> 00:02:34.620
It's just so cool to see
you both grow as artists.

00:02:34.620 --> 00:02:37.320
And as a fan, I'm just so pleased to be

00:02:37.320 --> 00:02:38.820
in a film that you're scoring.

00:02:38.820 --> 00:02:41.460
- It was clear that the film
was a lot bigger in scale

00:02:41.460 --> 00:02:43.170
than any of the others,
and so it was important

00:02:43.170 --> 00:02:46.440
that the sound, the music
stepped that up too,

00:02:46.440 --> 00:02:48.720
and fill that space, and really play

00:02:48.720 --> 00:02:51.690
into what a big summer movie needs to be,

00:02:51.690 --> 00:02:53.310
but in the world of Jordan Peele,

00:02:53.310 --> 00:02:54.570
which no one has seen yet.

00:02:54.570 --> 00:02:56.250
- Jordan has his style of filmmaking

00:02:56.250 --> 00:02:57.690
and you have your style of composing,

00:02:57.690 --> 00:03:01.140
and I feel like them together
is just ultimate power

00:03:01.140 --> 00:03:02.820
of artists coming together.

00:03:02.820 --> 00:03:04.170
- Well, I've got a question for you.

00:03:04.170 --> 00:03:06.870
As an actor, we haven't
met, even though we've been

00:03:06.870 --> 00:03:10.320
on socials, but I feel like I know you

00:03:10.320 --> 00:03:13.320
only because part of my job
is to watch your performance

00:03:13.320 --> 00:03:14.850
in the film as I'm scoring it.

00:03:14.850 --> 00:03:16.410
A lotta times I don't meet the actor

00:03:16.410 --> 00:03:18.660
until it's at the premier party.

00:03:18.660 --> 00:03:19.890
And I'm like, "Hey, how you doin'?"

00:03:19.890 --> 00:03:21.867
And they're like, "Who is this guy?"

00:03:22.740 --> 00:03:24.720
But I'm wondering when you see the film

00:03:24.720 --> 00:03:27.360
and you hear the music, like,
what does the music provide

00:03:27.360 --> 00:03:29.910
for an actor's experience of the film

00:03:29.910 --> 00:03:31.470
and what's that like for you?

00:03:31.470 --> 00:03:33.000
- Oh, score is so important.

00:03:33.000 --> 00:03:35.340
It's key for an audience member.

00:03:35.340 --> 00:03:37.950
And that's what's crazy
about being an actor.

00:03:37.950 --> 00:03:40.290
You're so locked in to
the dialog that goin' on.

00:03:40.290 --> 00:03:42.570
You're having a conversation,
you're acting, performing.

00:03:42.570 --> 00:03:43.800
All these emotions are running.

00:03:43.800 --> 00:03:45.990
But it's silent and then
when you hear scores

00:03:45.990 --> 00:03:48.180
subtly in there and then it kinda grows

00:03:48.180 --> 00:03:50.970
into this grand scale, it just
helps storytelling so much.

00:03:50.970 --> 00:03:53.550
And it's one of the key
components of filmmaking.

00:03:53.550 --> 00:03:55.530
There's times where it can go

00:03:55.530 --> 00:03:57.060
over the general public's head,

00:03:57.060 --> 00:03:59.400
like, they don't know
that that's even going on.

00:03:59.400 --> 00:04:01.590
But that's causing emotion for them.

00:04:01.590 --> 00:04:02.940
You know, it raises the stakes.

00:04:02.940 --> 00:04:04.740
I was watching dailies from "Nope"

00:04:04.740 --> 00:04:07.050
and where I'm just watching
little quick cuts and edits.

00:04:07.050 --> 00:04:08.557
And then it's without score and I'm like,

00:04:08.557 --> 00:04:09.690
"Oh, wow, this is great."

00:04:09.690 --> 00:04:10.650
Like, "Aw, amazing."

00:04:10.650 --> 00:04:12.600
And then score's involved and I'm like,

00:04:13.597 --> 00:04:14.640
"How'd this get better?"

00:04:14.640 --> 00:04:17.010
There's so many historical sounds

00:04:17.010 --> 00:04:18.690
that have been created through composing.

00:04:18.690 --> 00:04:21.030
And I feel like that's
what you've done with this.

00:04:21.030 --> 00:04:23.137
It's crazy hearing pieces
in this room, like,

00:04:23.137 --> 00:04:25.020
"Oh wow, these sounds are
gonna live on forever."

00:04:25.020 --> 00:04:28.800
- All of Jordan's movies
contain some kind of satire

00:04:28.800 --> 00:04:30.440
of something, you know.

00:04:30.440 --> 00:04:33.330
So the Jupiter's Claim theme
park was a great chance

00:04:33.330 --> 00:04:35.430
to provide a soundtrack
for this theme park

00:04:35.430 --> 00:04:36.690
that doesn't really exist.

00:04:36.690 --> 00:04:39.540
But in Jordan's world, we
play it like it's legit.

00:04:39.540 --> 00:04:42.120
So it was great to act
like that was a real thing.

00:04:42.120 --> 00:04:44.040
I feel really lucky when
I get to collaborate

00:04:44.040 --> 00:04:44.873
with him and help him.

00:04:44.873 --> 00:04:48.060
These crazy ideas that he
comes up with them them seem

00:04:48.060 --> 00:04:50.520
as real as they are when he thinks 'em up.

00:04:50.520 --> 00:04:54.990
- Do actors' performances
kind of inspire certain sounds

00:04:54.990 --> 00:04:56.520
or instruments that you like to bring in

00:04:56.520 --> 00:04:58.890
in order to display it, to
get a performance going?

00:04:58.890 --> 00:04:59.850
Like, is there anything that--?

00:04:59.850 --> 00:05:01.260
- Yeah, for sure.

00:05:01.260 --> 00:05:04.860
The actors, you know,
most of composer talks

00:05:04.860 --> 00:05:07.440
with the director about what
he or she wants for the film.

00:05:07.440 --> 00:05:11.880
Every director has a really
specific idea of music

00:05:11.880 --> 00:05:13.980
that they begin with and
they work with the composer

00:05:13.980 --> 00:05:16.620
to help find the unique
score to their film.

00:05:16.620 --> 00:05:19.200
Thinking about your performance,
like, your character.

00:05:19.200 --> 00:05:20.700
So we've never talked
about your character,

00:05:20.700 --> 00:05:24.330
but when I just watch your
performance, I get about him

00:05:24.330 --> 00:05:26.940
like, what a hopeful guy Angel is.

00:05:26.940 --> 00:05:30.270
I just feel like his life
is filled with possibility.

00:05:30.270 --> 00:05:32.190
He has just gone through a bad time,

00:05:32.190 --> 00:05:34.590
but now he's got this thing
that's fascinating him

00:05:34.590 --> 00:05:35.910
and he's really lit up by it.

00:05:35.910 --> 00:05:37.710
And I get that not in the script,

00:05:37.710 --> 00:05:40.830
it's just from, you know,
watching your performance.

00:05:40.830 --> 00:05:43.590
And I can't even explain how I feel that

00:05:43.590 --> 00:05:44.507
but it was from watching you.

00:05:44.507 --> 00:05:47.610
It was a couple of scenes
where Jordan's instructions

00:05:47.610 --> 00:05:49.710
to me was that it's a bad miracle

00:05:49.710 --> 00:05:51.660
and you gotta be able to have music

00:05:51.660 --> 00:05:55.557
that's one part, "Oh (beep),"
and one part, "oh my god."

00:05:56.850 --> 00:05:59.670
And so the music I'm doing
is trying to do that.

00:05:59.670 --> 00:06:02.280
The look on your face in
the couple of those scenes,

00:06:02.280 --> 00:06:03.600
I was like, that's the thing

00:06:03.600 --> 00:06:05.010
that I need to capture in music.

00:06:05.010 --> 00:06:06.780
What that actor's doing is the thing

00:06:06.780 --> 00:06:08.621
that's my job to reinforce.

00:06:08.621 --> 00:06:09.454
- It's crazy to hear this.

00:06:09.454 --> 00:06:11.670
This is so crazy for me
to hear all these details

00:06:11.670 --> 00:06:13.260
'cause I've never talked
to a composer ever.

00:06:13.260 --> 00:06:15.240
So this is just so cool to get these

00:06:15.240 --> 00:06:16.470
inside looks to everything.

00:06:16.470 --> 00:06:18.120
You played that line so well

00:06:18.120 --> 00:06:20.250
where you gave all these emotions

00:06:20.250 --> 00:06:22.410
and it was just so crazy to hear a score

00:06:22.410 --> 00:06:24.270
where it's like you're giving all hope.

00:06:24.270 --> 00:06:26.160
And then there's times
where you take it away.

00:06:26.160 --> 00:06:27.090
- Yep.
- In the sound.

00:06:27.090 --> 00:06:30.210
And it's so, it's like creepy-crawly

00:06:30.210 --> 00:06:31.350
like, ants on my back.

00:06:31.350 --> 00:06:33.510
I'm like, "Oh, that's
scary, that's scary."

00:06:33.510 --> 00:06:35.910
- Jordan really uses the slowdown

00:06:35.910 --> 00:06:40.470
of music and sound as a
warning sign to let you know

00:06:40.470 --> 00:06:43.057
that something really
bad's about to happen.

00:06:43.057 --> 00:06:45.060
- It's so cool to even be a part of one

00:06:45.060 --> 00:06:46.890
of the projects that you both are on.

00:06:46.890 --> 00:06:48.090
It's insane.

00:06:48.090 --> 00:06:49.980
I'm so glad to be having
this conversation with you.

00:06:49.980 --> 00:06:54.030
I think a lot of your work
is just, it lives on forever.

00:06:54.030 --> 00:06:56.370
Truly, like what you've
done with the score

00:06:56.370 --> 00:07:00.180
of "Get Out" especially,
it's just, it's so good.

00:07:00.180 --> 00:07:03.120
And it's just sounds that people
will resonate with forever.

00:07:03.120 --> 00:07:05.400
Those sounds that you've
crafted, those haunting sounds

00:07:05.400 --> 00:07:07.050
and those beats and drums.

00:07:07.050 --> 00:07:08.550
All those sounds together?

00:07:08.550 --> 00:07:09.960
It's gonna live on forever, truly.

00:07:09.960 --> 00:07:12.930
- One of the conversations
I have with Jordan is

00:07:12.930 --> 00:07:15.300
about rhythm because the
rhythms are really important.

00:07:15.300 --> 00:07:18.060
But you know, some rhythms
make you wanna dance

00:07:18.060 --> 00:07:21.270
and some let you know that
something bad's about to happen

00:07:21.270 --> 00:07:23.010
and maybe you oughta run and not dance.

00:07:23.010 --> 00:07:25.260
The difference between those
is kinda slight sometimes,

00:07:25.260 --> 00:07:27.810
so I'm always, when I'm
coming up with rhythms

00:07:27.810 --> 00:07:30.870
I wanna be ones that make
people wanna dance a little.

00:07:30.870 --> 00:07:34.193
And sometimes he'll say, "Mm,
that's not scary enough."

00:07:34.193 --> 00:07:35.026
You know?
- Oh, yeah?

00:07:35.026 --> 00:07:36.270
- And I have to go back and I'll make it

00:07:36.270 --> 00:07:37.590
less dance and more scary.

00:07:37.590 --> 00:07:39.780
- I love the ones that
are kind of both though,

00:07:39.780 --> 00:07:41.940
where it's like you have that rhythmic.

00:07:41.940 --> 00:07:43.500
It's like, "Oh, it's comin', it's comin'."

00:07:43.500 --> 00:07:44.730
Then it's just like, "Nevermind."

00:07:44.730 --> 00:07:46.440
Like, "Oh, okay, I gotta go, I gotta go."

00:07:46.440 --> 00:07:47.490
- Okay, well I'm right with you

00:07:47.490 --> 00:07:49.710
because I think that dance
rhythms can be scary too.

00:07:49.710 --> 00:07:51.240
This has been such a pleasure, I just.

00:07:51.240 --> 00:07:52.710
- Yeah, cheers man.
- Just thank you.

00:07:52.710 --> 00:07:54.210
- Thank you for chatting with me.

00:07:54.210 --> 00:07:57.570
It's great that our first
time meeting is documented.

00:07:57.570 --> 00:08:00.120
And yeah, I'm just so grateful to be here.

00:08:00.120 --> 00:08:01.110
It's crazy to me, I'm talking

00:08:01.110 --> 00:08:03.330
to Michael Abel, "Nope" is here.

00:08:03.330 --> 00:08:07.080
In the movie, it's a dream,
this is bigger than a dream.

00:08:07.080 --> 00:08:09.540
Like, you know, our movie
thing is a bad miracle,

00:08:09.540 --> 00:08:11.973
this is the greatest
miracle of all time for me.

